  "suite": "device_pop",
  "version": 1,
  "description": "ADR 0022 (PROTOCOL 3.1, 3.4): a device certificate's proof of possession. pop = Ed25519 by the DEVICE key over utf8(\"z-device-pop-v1:\") || account_ed (32) || device_ed (32) || device_x (32) || utf8(device_id), as an optional member of the certificate. No signature the account makes covers it, so a list or zc2. code with it and the same one without have the same signatures and the same fingerprint, and a reader from before it admits both. A reader that knows it refuses a certificate whose pop is present and does not verify — and with it the list or code that carries it. A certificate proves possession when it verifies and either carries a pop or its device key IS the account key (device #1, a legacy record), where sig is already the device's own signature. What a receiver then does with a list that names a device proven for another account, or names a device it has seen proven without the proof, is §3.4's receiver rule: state, not a property of the bytes, so not vectored here.",
